# Citation File Format 1.2.0. GitHub renders the "Cite this repository" widget
# from this file, and the Zenodo GitHub integration mints a DOI per release from
# this same metadata.
#
# There is deliberately no `.zenodo.json` beside it (Section 7 item 6): Zenodo
# prefers that file when both exist, so adding one would silently override
# everything here and leave two descriptions of one release that have to agree.
#
# Once the first DOI is minted, add it as the `doi:` key below and to the README
# badge row. It is not here yet because a DOI that has not been minted is a
# citation that does not resolve.

cff-version: 1.2.0
message: If you use this software, please cite it using these metadata.
title: "ML Experiment Triage"
abstract: >-
  A tool that turns a directory of machine learning training runs into a ranked,
  significance tested comparison against a baseline. Two sided permutation
  testing on the mean of a smoothed final window, in a seed replicated mode and
  a weaker single run window block mode that is labelled as such everywhere,
  with Benjamini Hochberg correction in one family per mode and a practical
  effect gate beside the statistical one. The tool's own error rates are
  measured by a calibration suite rather than assumed, and every published rate
  is rendered from one source.
type: software
authors:
  - family-names: Badejo
    given-names: Olajide
version: 1.1.0
date-released: "2026-08-31"
license: MIT
repository-code: https://github.com/Olajide-Badejo/ML-Experiment-Triage
url: https://olajide-badejo.github.io/ML-Experiment-Triage/
keywords:
  - machine learning
  - mlops
  - experiment tracking
  - statistics
  - hypothesis testing
  - permutation test
  - multiple comparisons
  - reproducibility
